/decrement numeric value  | O(1)\nSTRLEN  | Get string length  | O(1)\nGETRANGE  | Get substring  | O(N) where N is the length of the returned string\nSETRANGE  | Set substring  | O(N) where N is the length of the substring\n\nSources: tests/unit/type/list.tcl1-40\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nData Encoding Internals | fluent/fluent-bit | DeepWiki (https://deepwiki.com/fluent/fluent-bit/2.8-data-encoding-internals)\nciteturn69search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Simple Dynamic Strings (SDS)Fluent Bit implements a clone of the SDS library for efficient string handling, as covered in `flb_sds`.\n## 4. Simple Dynamic Strings (SDS)\n\nFluent Bit implements a clone of the SDS library for efficient string handling, as covered in `flb_sds`.\n\n### Features\n\n  * Memory-efficient, length-prefixed strings reducing buffer reallocations.\n  * Headers contain allocated size and current length.\n  * Supports dynamic resizing on concatenation or copying.\n  * Utilities for trimming whitespace, copying, appending with escape characters.\n  * Safe variants that update callers' pointers on reallocation.\n\n### Interface Highlights\n\n  * Creation: `flb_sds_create_len()`, `flb_sds_create()`, `flb_sds_create_size()`\n  * Manipulation: `flb_sds_cat()`, `flb_sds_copy()`, `flb_sds_trim()`, `flb_sds_cat_esc()`\n  * Memory Safety: Built-in tracking of capacity and length.\n\nSDS strings are pervasive across fluent-bit for fields like tags, file paths, and user properties.\n\n## 5. Hash Tables and Hash Functions\n\nMultiple internal modules use hash tables primarily for fast key-value lookups, e.g., in environment variables handling (`flb_env`), and cryptographic hashes for validation or signing.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nts-antirez-sds - npm Package Security Analysis - Socket (https://socket.dev/npm/package/ts-antirez-sds)\nciteturn69search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; ts-antirez-sds\n## Acknowledgements\n\n  * Salvatore Sanfilippo (antirez) - author of sds, the original C library, and of Redis.\n  * sds contributors - ongoing maintenance of the C library.\n\n## Keywords\n\nsds\n\ndynamic-strings\n\nredis\n\nstring\n\nc-to-ts\n\ntypescript\n\nWhat is ts-antirez-sds?\n\nSimple Dynamic Strings (the C string library used by Redis), translated to TypeScript\n\nIs ts-antirez-sds well maintained?\n\nWe found that ts-antirez-sds demonstrated a healthy version release cadence and project activity because the last version was released less than a year ago. It has 1 open source maintainer collaborating on the project.\n\nPackage last updated on 05 Sep 2026--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nString Handling: SDS and Extensions | jcorporation/mympd | DeepWiki (https://deepwiki.com/jcorporation/mympd/6.1-string-handling%3A-sds-and-extensions)\nciteturn69search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; myMPD relies on the Simple Dynamic Strings (sds) library for robust, binary-safe, and efficient string manipulation.\n\n# String Handling: SDS and Extensions\n\nRelevant source files\n\n  * dist/rax/rax.c\n  * dist/rax/rax.h\n  * dist/sds/sds.c\n  * dist/sds/sds.h\n  * src/lib/config/handle_options.c\n  * src/lib/config/state_files.c\n  * src/lib/mem.c\n  * src/lib/mem.h\n  * src/lib/sds/sds_extras.c\n  * src/lib/sds/sds_extras.h\n  * src/lib/sds/sds_file.c\n  * src/lib/sds/sds_file.h\n  * src/lib/utf8_wrapper.c\n  * src/lib/utf8_wrapper.h\n  * src/mympd_api/albums.c\n  * src/mympd_api/tags.c\n  * test/tests/test_sds_extras.c\n  * test/tests/test_utf8wrap.c\n\nmyMPD relies on the Simple Dynamic Strings (sds) library for robust, binary-safe, and efficient string manipulation. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSDS (Simple Dynamic Strings) and LLRB Tree | fonttools/ttfautohint | DeepWiki (https://deepwiki.com/fonttools/ttfautohint/7.2-sds-%28simple-dynamic-strings%29-and-llrb-tree)\nciteturn69search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # SDS (Simple Dynamic Strings) and LLRB Tree ... This section details the two primary data structure utility libraries used within `ttfautohint`: SDS for dynamic string management and LLRB for efficient balanced tree operations.\n\n# SDS (Simple Dynamic Strings) and LLRB Tree\n\nRelevant source files\n\n  * lib/llrb.h\n  * lib/sds-wrapper.c\n  * lib/sds.c\n  * lib/sds.h\n\nThis section details the two primary data structure utility libraries used within `ttfautohint`: SDS for dynamic string management and LLRB for efficient balanced tree operations. These libraries provide the foundation for metadata generation, control instruction lookups, and bytecode optimization.\n\n## Simple Dynamic Strings (SDS)\n\n`ttfautohint` utilizes the SDS library (originally from the Redis project) to handle variable-length strings. Unlike standard C strings, SDS strings are binary-safe, store their length explicitly, and use a specialized header to allow for $O(1)$ length lookups and efficient memory reallocations.\n\n### Data Structure and Memory Layout\n\nAn SDS string consists of a header (`sdshdr`) followed immediately by the character buffer. The pointer returned to the user points directly to the `buf` member, allowing SDS strings to be passed to standard C library functions like `printf` or `strlen`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow Redis SDS (Simple Dynamic Strings) Works (https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-03-31-redis-sds-simple-dynamic-strings/view)\nciteturn69search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Instead, it uses a custom string implementation called SDS (Simple Dynamic Strings).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n#1118690 - ITP: sds -- Simple Dynamic Strings library for C - Debian Bug report logs (https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi